A||||15||GWMORGAN||replacing dog-bone bushing||||1
Z||000000||GWMORGAN||10-03-1999||11:03 PM||||Has anyone replaced the stock dog-bone bushing with the urethane from the fiero store? If so, how hard/easy is this procedure. Info will greatly appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||10-03-1999||11:27 PM||||I did it . it was no problem.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||10-04-1999||12:54 AM||||Some people have had problems getting the old ones out, and the fit of the new, So I saved myself the trouble and just bought the Ultimate Dog Bone?  One out - One in 15min tops with two people. <P>"And thats the bottom line because Stone Cold Said So"  I always wanted to say that  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||mshill||10-04-1999||03:18 AM||||I had problems with the bushings fitting, but I obviously didn't have a stock V6 dogbone to begin with.<P>I you got the right one, removing the bushings is the hardest part.  After that it's a piece of cake.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-04-1999||09:17 AM||||You have to have the original dog bone. What I call the type 1 or old style. The type 2 bone will not accept the replacement bushings because of the tapered rib inside the loops. <P>The type 1 bone has no taper inside the loop. It's reinforcment is on the outside of the loops. <P>I've talked to the nice people at Suspension.com, he's told me that Prothane will not even acknowledge that their is a type 2 bone.<P>I have a set of bushings comming, and will post a set of pictures of the 2 bones soon.<P>seems to me removing the type 1 bushings should be easy. thread a hack saw or coping saw blade thru the free space in the loop and cut the ear off the bushing. hard part is finding a really coarse blade for a hack saw. At least that's what I'm going to try.... I'll let you know how it works.||||reg||4
Z||000005||GWMORGAN||10-04-1999||03:16 PM||||Just had a look at the stock dog-bone. It's the one that has a ridge running along it's center, so I think the replacement urthane bushing should fit perfectly. Thanks for the info guys.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||10-04-1999||05:38 PM||||FOCOA has both.||||reg||1
Z||000007||TOM||10-04-1999||05:47 PM||||To get the old bushings out just lay the bone down on the vise and hit the bushing with a hammer. They drive right out.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mshill||10-05-1999||12:51 AM||||OK, I had a type 1 (urethane bushings won't fit).  I got a type 2 but it is 1" shorter and I'll be darned if I can't get the engine to rotate that 1" to get the bolt in.<P>I just spent 30 minutes with my three boys (granted they aren't very big) but  with the brake off and the tranny in 2nd they were pushing on the front of the car getting it to bounce so that the engine would rotate.  With them pushing and me in the trunk pulling on the manifold we couldn't get it to move more than 1/2 inch.  Must have been funny to watch thought, as I am sitting in the trunk with the rear deck lid (with wing) pounding me in the head as I try to pull the engine back and shove a bolt through a little hole.<P>ARE WE SURE THERE WAS ONLY ONE SIZE DOGBONE FOR THE V6??? Help!!!!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||10-06-1999||08:11 PM||||Tom, unless your bone was completely seperated from the bushings,,,,, you ain't driving it out.<P>I didn't have to cut it out. I was able to ply it loose, but it was still frimly bonded to the loop. Left chunks behind that I had to cut out.<P>mshill, It's entirely posible they changed the flaming thing. BTW, you got the types backward but I got the point  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> My sugestion is go back to the salvage yard you got the thing from and measure the brackets and position. That should confirm it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||TOM||10-06-1999||09:02 PM||||Hey orge. Its was a little beat but i used a real big hammer. <BR>hey mshill. I put the new one in by myself. I used a bar to pull the engine back. Mine moved real easy probably because all the other mounts are shot. I will be getting to those shortly.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||10-07-1999||09:30 AM||||some warning for those considering the urethane bushings on a 4 cyl......<P>All the vibration previously absorbed in the sloppy bushings will go straight to the body. You will find out quickly just how rough a 4 cyl really idles, even when it's idleing correctly.<P>I expected increased vibration, but even then it was a bit of a suprise how much diferance there is... The bushings will break in a bit, but only a bit.<P>On the other hand,,,,, my air cleaner no longer hits the trunk wall when I stand on it.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dave85||10-07-1999||10:57 AM||||My bushing were easy as pie to change. I just took a hammer and screwdriver and knocked the metal sleave out and the bushing popped right out with a little prying with the srcewdriver. The whole job took about 15 minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000013||mshill||10-08-1999||02:17 AM||||Tom,<P>Got mine in finally.  Took a pair of floorjacks under the exhaust pipes just infront of the rear wheels to tilt it enough. After that it was easy.<P>Mark S.||||reg||1
Z||000014||GWMORGAN||10-08-1999||04:04 AM||||Installed the Fiero Store's dog bone bushing last nite. Had to use a pry-bar and pried (take stress off dog-bone) the engine a little in order to get dog-bone out. Now there's no slop when I plant the tires.BIIIG difference.<BR>    BTW, used screw driver and hammer to get old bushing out.||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||10-08-1999||10:42 AM||||Well I sat down and looked at the mounts for all the trannys again last night.... only the 86.5+ auto tranny with the 4 cyl uses the 2 point mounting arangement.... How annoying.... <P>I'm guessing that I'm getting a hell of allot more viration than those with the 3 point mounts.... <P>With the 3 point mount the motors own mass has to work against gravity and climb ontop of a mount to rotate. With the 2 point mount the motor is directly over both mounts, and only the strut keeps it from falling over. With the 3 point mount the motor only really loads the strut when the car is moving, the 2 point setup loads the strut heavily at all times. The width of the three point setup acts like adding addtion struts to the motor.<P>with these 2 point mount sets, the the urethane is annoying but nessiary. I had a brand new type 2 strut, and for the gigles I tryed a used type one in good shape. The air cleaner was trying to hit the body with both. I supose one way I could do this would be urethane only one end of the type 1 bone, and leave rubber in the other. that way I'd get some vibration isolation, and still restrict movement.<P>I'm working on another page for the cave about all this.... Should be up, with some pitcures, in the next week or so.||||reg||1
